  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=3{t}^{6}-2{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-7t,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{cm/min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{6}-2{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-7t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{5}-8{t}^{3}-16t-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =18(1)^{5}-8(1)^{3}-16(1)-7 \\\\  & =18-8-16-7 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-13|=13\\,\\text{cm/min}.$",
